\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces mAP over every labeled box, by the amount of recurrent history the released streaming evaluation gives the frame. Raw $\Delta $mAP is the full column minus the short one, the quantity main Fig.~2 plots, so positive means longer history scored higher. The last column subtracts the placebo trend from it, using the mean of the other two RVT rows for an RVT row and the mean of all three for an SSM row. The RVT rows are the placebos: their state crosses chunk boundaries, so position carries no support for them. Net of that control the SSM rows gain 4.37{} and 5.32{} points ($z=7.2$ and $10.0$), against at most 0.67{} points in magnitude ($z=1.56$) for any placebo. The short and full columns carry three decimals, so differencing them reproduces the raw column exactly. Cluster bootstrap over 406{} sequences.}}{9}{table.caption.27}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:chunk}{{4}{9}{mAP over every labeled box, by the amount of recurrent history the released streaming evaluation gives the frame. Raw $\Delta $mAP is the full column minus the short one, the quantity main Fig.~2 plots, so positive means longer history scored higher. The last column subtracts the placebo trend from it, using the mean of the other two RVT rows for an RVT row and the mean of all three for an SSM row. The RVT rows are the placebos: their state crosses chunk boundaries, so position carries no support for them. Net of that control the SSM rows gain \chunkDidSmall {} and \chunkDidBase {} points ($z=\chunkDidSmallZ $ and $\chunkDidBaseZ $), against at most \chunkPlaceboMax {} points in magnitude ($z=\chunkPlaceboZ $) for any placebo. The short and full columns carry three decimals, so differencing them reproduces the raw column exactly. Cluster bootstrap over \outSeqsScanned {} sequences}{table.caption.27}{}}
\newlabel{tab:chunk@cref}{{[table][4][]4}{[1][9][]9}{}{}{}}
